    "title": "MTA Says Agreement Near on LIRR Talks - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Pam Robinson ,\n6 hours ago The Metropolitan Transportation Authority said Wednesday that it is optimistic that a deal can be reached with five Long Island Rail Road unions before Saturday’s strike deadline.\nAt a press conference, Gary Dellaverson, MTA labor negotiator, said that the agency had made an offer of $134 million to prevent the first LIRR strike since 1994, when workers struck for two days. But that figure includes a lump sum payment,which some union members object, saying that the payments wouldn’t become a permanent part of their contracts.\nMeanwhile, Gov. Kathy Hochul urged both sides to find a solution to prevent a disruption of service for thousands of Long Island commuters who rely on trains to reach their jobs in New York City.\nIf a strike, scheduled to start at 12:01 a.m. Saturday, continues into Monday, Huntington Station would become a hub for shuttle service as one of a handful of stations designated to transport workers into Queens. From there, commuters would have to connect to subway service to continue their travel.\n“No one wants a strike. I don’t want a strike,” Hochul said, saying she didn’t want “unnecessary fare hikes or higher taxes.”\nDellaverson said the figure the MTA proposed matched exactly what was recommended by a federal panel of mediators.\nThe unions that could strike represent about 3,500 workers,including, signalmen, engineers, trainmen, electricians, and machinists.\nThe two sides are scheduled to continue negotiations on Thursday.\nDellaverson, right, takes questions at a press conference.\nLIRR Strike Could Turn Huntington Station Into Shuttle Hub for Commuters\n1",
    "summary": "1 hours ago The Metropolitan Transportation Authority said Wednesday that it is optimistic that a deal can be reached with five Long Island Rail Road unions before Saturday’s strike deadline. At a press conference, Gary Dellaverson, MTA labor negotiator, said that the agency had made an offer of $134 million to prevent the first LIRR strike since 1994, when workers struck for two days. Kathy Hochul urged both sides to find a solution to prevent a disruption of service for thousands of Long Island commuters who rely on trains to reach their jobs in New York City.",
